{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat collab/yang-base/1795186644731-architect-yang-preludes-design-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/yang-base/1795169993265-architect-yang-preludes-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-20 15:04:07 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-20 22:04:07 +07 (+0700)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1f878-9255-7591-a9d7-8c528b565999\n# Task: Architecture review r2 confirmation of docs/design/yang.preludes.md (all 10 r1 findings reconciled)\nRole: Lead System Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6-astra | Assigned: 2026-11-20 21:57:24 +07 (+0700) | Status: completed | Rationale: Resume same OpenAI GPT Lead System Architect session (01a1f878-9255-7591-a9d7-8c528b565999) for r2 confirmation review of reconciled design\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-20 15:02:23 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-20 22:02:23 ICT\n\n**1. Verdict: REVISE**\n\nFive r1 findings are **CONFIRMED-FIXED**; five remain **OPEN**. The architecture remains viable, but several revised contracts contradict live primitives or other requirements in the document.\n\nI re-read the full revised design and the r1 report, and inspected supporting implementation contracts. This was a read-only review; no files were edited and no repository tests were run. Findings below concern specified behavior, not the absence of proposed implementations.\n\n**2. r1 finding-by-finding disposition**\n\nLine references in this table refer to `docs/design/yang.preludes.md`.\n\n| # | r1 Finding | Status | Evidence / Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Portable numeric semantics | **OPEN** | Qualified adapters and integer kernels are the right correction. However, the classifier at line 522 rejects CLJS float carriers, and lines 527–530 violate or omit the newly documented helper preconditions. See F1. |\n| 2 | Frontend-owned Hook ABI and structural lowering | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Lines 317–328 keep language-specific contracts outside core. Lines 539–551 specify compatible globals, function construction, control hooks, and unit-body arguments. Missing hooks and generator-expression gating are addressed. The separate function-entry defect is covered under finding 3. |\n| 3 | Callable admission and exception bridging | **OPEN** | Authenticated cells and private provenance forwarding are appropriate, but the claimed behavior of `yang.call/eligible?` is incorrect. The entry boundary also loses normal return values and misclassifies binding failures. See F2–F3. |\n| 4 | Portable `clojure.core` publication | **OPEN** | Export inventory, excluded host-dependent names, new helpers, primitive projections, task initialization, and sibling isolation are now explicit. The selected definitions still contain nonportable arithmetic, and dynamically returned functions lack a compatible construction contract. See F4–F5. |\n| 5 | Generator frames and context migration | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Lines 140–151 preserve open non-`:finally` dispatch and distinguish frame count from activation depth. §4.5 and P4 now distinguish Python rebasing from PHP context restoration. |\n| 6 | Hygienic hooks, authority validation, bundled helpers | **OPEN** | Qualification, lookup precedence, exact export validation, and reserved-door exclusions are corrected. However, the proposed bundled `get` binding cannot read vector frames when source code defines `get`. See F6. |\n| 7 | Float rounding, domains, and capture safety | **OPEN** | Both exponentiation orders and their regression witness are preserved. Domain declarations improve substantially, but policy-hook signatures still pass raw doubles across the boundary prohibited by line 184. See F7. |\n| 8 | Integer v4 refusal algebra | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Lines 210–219 correctly distinguish the three returned reasons from operational throws, preserve PHP-owned demotion, and replace implicit whitespace policy with `skip-while`. |\n| 9 | Recipe/profile separation and verified closure | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | Root-address pinning, immutable verified artifacts, qualified collision rules, and an optional effect ceiling resolve the architectural concerns. The empty-ceiling example needs a small correction; see F8. |\n| 10 | Roadmap and acceptance gates | **CONFIRMED-FIXED** | P0 preserves existing emission details; P2 preserves default lowering bytes; P3 leaves default Clojure bundling unchanged. Independent P1 validation precedes coordinated activation. Gates are substantially more concrete, although they must incorporate the remaining corrections below. |\n\nConfirmed sound at the design level: explicit caller-owned state in `yrt.*`; no core language registry; separation of parsing, lowering, admission, publication, and execution; private Layer-2b provenance handling; schema-1 preservation with call-site metadata outside manifests; deterministic emission; and unchanged default Clojure publication during P3. These are design confirmations, not execution-parity certifications.\n\n**3. New or remaining findings**\n\n**F1 — HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:522](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:522) | Numeric classification and dispatch are not portable as specified | Define an executable kind test and complete guarded dispatch.**\n\nThe proposed classifier first requires `(data/number? x)`. The live [implementation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc:418) recognizes host numbers and exact-integer carriers. It does **not** recognize the CLJS [`Float64` object](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/host/number.cljc:76). Consequently, a boxed float reaches the nonnumeric branch on Node. The subsequent `data/content=` test never runs. Copying the existing TypeScript expression does not establish its correctness.\n\nThere are additional dispatch contradictions:\n\n- Line 527 converts both operands of negative integer exponentiation to floats, but `neg-pow` requires a **positive exact integer** exponent.\n- Line 530 routes general float remainder to `fmod-pos`, whose domain excludes negative operands, zero divisors, and nonfinite values.\n- Float exponentiation lacks the required exact-integral exponent check or explicit refusal of fractional exponents.\n- Falling back to host-backed Yin `=` for collections leaves nested float equality host-dependent. CLJS `Float64` equality distinguishes signed zeros and equates NaNs; scalar special cases do not repair collection comparisons.\n\nSpecify portable carrier recognition, preserve exact exponent magnitudes, and define each sign/range/nonfinite branch or its explicit guest refusal. Add cross-host cases for boxed `1.0`, `2 ** -3`, negative remainder operands, fractional exponents, and collections containing signed zeros or NaNs.\n\n**F2 — HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:565](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:565) | The entry boundary loses results and violates binding-exception semantics | Use a value-returning boundary around binding and execution.**\n\nLine 566 executes the function body through `try-orelse` with `identity` as `orelse`. By the document’s own line 163—and live [Python implementation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:317)—normal execution discards the body result and calls `(orelse)` with **zero arguments**. This cannot preserve a function’s return value, including the promised result `20`.\n\nLine 565 also specifies direct `:yang.call/arity` transfer for parameter mismatch. [§8.10.7](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3861) requires callee binding failures to retain their local exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. The boundary must be installed **before binding**, as the existing [Clojure entry](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1218) does.\n\nUse `yrt.escape/try-value` for function entries; retain `try-orelse` for source `try/except/else`. Put parameter binding inside the entry boundary and distinguish malformed protocol records from ordinary callee binding errors.\n\n**F3 — HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:563](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:563) | Heap authentication is incorrectly attributed to common callable admission | Specify source-level admission before structural invocation.**\n\n`yang.call/eligible?` does not reject all unauthenticated maps. Its [descriptor projection](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:29) dereferences an authenticated cell reference but otherwise retains the original map. A map with an entry and no eligibility predicate can therefore pass structural eligibility.\n\nClojure’s `dsg__admitted?` separately checks authenticated cells or its private immutable-descriptor witness. Those are different contracts.\n\nDefine the admission step performed by `clj.py/call` and source-facing callable predicates before invoking the shared helper. Preserve admissible foreign callables while refusing source data that merely contains entry-shaped keys. Do not change `yang.call` into a closed language registry or claim that structural eligibility establishes source authority.\n\n**F4 — HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:580](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:580) | The “portable subset” retains nonportable numeric definitions | Adapt or restrict numeric library exports.**\n\nThe selected library includes `inc`, `dec`, and `rem`. Live [`inc` and `dec`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2608) use bare `+` and `-`; the [VM registry](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:404) binds these to host arithmetic. Cell-backed callable wrapping changes admission, not arithmetic.\n\nThus projected `inc(1.0)` still reaches the carrier-coercion problem that replacing Python operator hooks was intended to solve. Exact-integer arithmetic also remains outside the declared kernel/limit policy.\n\nSpecify portable implementations or explicit operand restrictions for these exports, including their equality behavior. Keep the correction confined to the new library profile so the existing Clojure default remains unchanged.\n\n**F5 — MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:586](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:586) | Initialization covers exported callables but not dynamically returned functions | Define callable construction throughout the new library.**\n\nThe library exports [`comp` and `partial`](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2887), which construct functions during invocation. Existing Clojure [function compilation](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1299) produces immutable descriptors with unit-local admission witnesses. Allocating top-level export cells in `init!` does not change those returned functions.\n\nThis is an unresolved publication contract, rather than a demand that the implementation already exist.\n\nSpecify how all escaping functions receive compatible admission at construction time. Preserve function identity across passage; avoid receiver-side replacement wrappers. If the new library uses a different compiler construction mode, reconcile its internal call path too: the existing intra-unit dispatcher reads an entry directly from a descriptor map.\n\nAdd a gate that returns `comp`/`partial` results and invokes them from independently compiled Clojure and other frontend units.\n\n**F6 — HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:172](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:172) | The existing `dsg__get` fallback cannot inspect escape frames | Supply capture-safe vector access.**\n\nWhen source code defines `get`, the existing [`dsg__get` fallback](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2484) scans maps and returns `nil` for nonmaps. Escape frames are vectors, and the new runtime explicitly reads their indexed slots.\n\nBinding shared escape-runtime `get` directly to that helper therefore breaks frame dispatch and traversal in this supported shadowing case. This is a concrete mismatch in the proposed reuse plan.\n\nExtend the portable helper to cover the required vector semantics, or use a dedicated guest frame accessor built from `data/nth`. Test bundled/linked parity with source-defined `get` and active exception/finally frames.\n\n**F7 — MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:201](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:201) | Policy-hook signatures contradict the raw-double capture rule | Separate raw computation from callback invocation.**\n\nLine 184 prohibits arbitrary guest calls while unwrapped doubles remain live. Nevertheless, lines 201–202 specify raw-double `x` and invoke `(on-underflow x n)` or `(on-range x)`.\n\nCalling the location a “guard boundary” does not make that argument or the suspended environment portable.\n\nDefine the boundary concretely: raw helpers should return boxed results or safe status data, then a wrapper should invoke policy closures after leaving the raw arithmetic frame. Alternatively, provide an equally explicit capture-safe protocol. Cover exceptional hook paths in the P1 gate, not only successful arithmetic.\n\n**F8 — LOW | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:279](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:279) | The example effect ceiling rejects its own runtime | Omit the ceiling or provide an intentional allowed set.**\n\nThe Python profile example specifies `:yang.prelude/max-effects #{}`. Under line 296’s subset rule, this rejects a runtime using cell effects, including `cell/new`.\n\nUse the documented absent/unbounded form for this example, or an explicit sufficient ceiling. This is an example defect; the revised ceiling architecture itself is sound.\n\n**4. Owner-decision framing: D1–D7**\n\n| Decision | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| **D1 — Local mutation** | Concur with task-local cells and a separately named single-assignment variant. This fits current lowering and `nonlocal` requirements. |\n| **D2 — `yrt.*` naming** | Concur. Linker names and implementation namespaces are clearly distinguished and align with the related designs. |\n| **D3 — Python float tags** | Concur with retaining tags during P1. Representation migration should remain separate from runtime extraction. |\n| **D4 — Batched activation** | Conditional concurrence remains appropriate: validate each extraction independently, then coordinate activation and refresh. Golden changes must follow semantic evidence. |\n| **D5 — Curated projections** | Concur. Explicit aliases, lexical precedence, exact export checks, and reserved-door bans provide a bounded extension surface. |\n| **D6 — Standalone `clojure.core`** | Concur with the constrained direction and unchanged default Clojure bundling. Implementation readiness remains conditional on F4–F5 and the callable-boundary corrections. |\n| **D7 — Two exponentiation orders** | Concur with retaining both algorithms for P1. Unification remains a separate owner-approved semantic change with an oracle-departure record. |\n\nThese remain **owner decisions**, not approvals implied by “Architect concurred.” The revised framing is appropriate; the remaining work is to reconcile the concrete contracts and their acceptance tests.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-20 10:27:52 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-20 17:27:52 +07 (+0700)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1f878-9255-7591-a9d7-8c528b565999\n# Task: Architecture review of docs/design/yang.preludes.md (configurable and composable Yang preludes)\nRole: Lead System Architect\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6-astra | Assigned: 2026-11-20 17:19:53 +07 (+0700) | Status: completed | Rationale: Independent different-family Lead System Architect review (OpenAI GPT reviewing Gemini-authored design) per docs/agents/team.md and docs/agents/roles/architect.md\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-20 10:25:52 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-20 17:25:52 ICT\n\n**1. Verdict: REVISE**\n\nThe shared-runtime decomposition is architecturally viable. The proposed Python-to-Clojure profile, however, contains incompatible numeric, calling, binding, and publication contracts. Several P1 extraction assumptions also contradict live code.\n\nThese are design defects requiring reconciliation before implementation, not objections that proposed namespaces do not yet exist. P4’s deferral is reasonable, but P1 must preserve existing generators.\n\nReview was read-only. Evidence comprises source inspection and a small, in-memory numerical reproduction; repository test suites were not run.\n\n**2. Findings**\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:287](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:287) | The Clojure profile’s numeric hooks violate four-host portability | Define a portable numeric contract and adapters.**\n\nThe descriptor emits float64 carriers but maps arithmetic and comparisons directly to bare `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and comparison primitives. `yang.antlr.md` §8.5.5 explicitly states that generic arithmetic on decoded carriers is not portable: Node refuses carrier coercion while JVM/Dart can compute. Thus even `2.0 + 1.0` contradicts P3’s portability claim.\n\nThe “exact integer” claim also needs more than `clj.py/int-lit`: subsequent operations must preserve exactness and obey integer limits. [yin.vm.integer:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:9) requires guest integer arithmetic through its kernels.\n\nSpecify operand kinds, mixed numeric operations, equality, division results, overflow/refusal behavior, and float reboxing. Alternatively, explicitly refuse unsupported numeric operations in this profile.\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:310](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:310) | Hook substitution does not define a compatible lowering ABI | Specify frontend-owned hook signatures and structural lowering rules.**\n\nThe proposed mappings are not interchangeable with current Python calls:\n\n- Python global reads/writes pass a module dictionary and name; `clj/global-get` and `clj/define-global!` take only a name, or name/value.\n- Python `try` takes body, handler, and `orelse`; `clj/try` takes two arguments. Direct replacement loses `try/except/else` behavior.\n- Python’s unit body receives `%globals` and `%globals-fn`; `clj/run-main` invokes a zero-argument body and removes one `[:yin.k/ok value]` wrapper.\n- Python function construction includes parameter specifications, definition-time defaults, keyword defaults, and an argument-vector body.\n\nSee [Python lowering:801](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:801), [function-value:1836](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1836), and [Clojure runtime:136](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:136).\n\nThe example also uses `%`, but the descriptor supplies no remainder hook. Comprehensions, exception matching, unpacking, and parameter-binding capabilities need explicit support or refusal.\n\nKeep these contracts in the installed frontend implementation or a frontend-supplied contract description. Making Yang core understand Python constructs would undermine the open SPI.\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:390](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:390) | Shared entry shape does not establish Clojure callable admission or exception bridging | Specify trusted constructor and call-boundary behavior.**\n\nExisting Clojure functions are not merely maps with `:yang.call/entry`. The compiler constructs admission witnesses and private invocation tokens; dynamic calls validate admission. See [descriptor construction:1282](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1282) and [admission checking:2409](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2409). A new immutable `clj.py` descriptor does not automatically satisfy separately compiled `clojure.core` consumers.\n\nLikewise, passing `clj/raise` directly as the common raise exit is insufficient. It accepts a local thrown value, whereas `yang.call` transfers a condition. Existing [compile-guest-call:1427](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1427) unwraps ordinary thrown payloads and privately retains protocol-condition provenance.\n\nDefine admission across independently compiled units, public invocation-record handling, binding-before-body exception protection, and condition conversion. Keep provenance operations inside trusted generated Layer-2b boundaries. Require tests for forged descriptors, condition-shaped thrown data, retained protocol exceptions, and A→B→A cleanup.\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:427](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:427) | Publishing the entire existing library is neither a complete export plan nor a portable module plan | Publish an explicit portable surface with task-instantiation rules.**\n\n`yang.clojure/library` is quoted source currently selected transitively by `library-for`. Publishing all of it changes admission requirements: `resolve` references unavailable `kind/resolve`, and `var?` requires `kind/var?`, which the supplied registry installs only on CLJ. See [kind-module:2578](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2578) and [library:2677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2677).\n\nSeveral promised names also are not definitions in that library: `empty?` is a VM primitive, while `assoc-in`, `update-in`, `set`, and `hash-map` are not supplied by the cited library/primitive surfaces.\n\nSpecify the export inventory, primitive-to-callable projections, portable subset, qualified sibling references, initialization, and per-task callable construction. Preserve library-internal bindings against caller global redefinition. Linking a module must not implicitly project all its exports into a source namespace.\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:135](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:135) | The escape-frame contract excludes existing generator boundaries | Preserve generator frames during P1.**\n\nThe proposed frame kind is restricted to `:handler` or `:finally`. Both [Python gen-start:466](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:466) and [PHP gen-start:5309](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5309) install `:generator` frames today. Existing raise logic dispatches every non-finally frame to its payload.\n\nEither retain that handler-equivalent behavior or explicitly migrate generator boundary construction to the new representation. Deferring generator extraction does not defer compatibility.\n\nAlso make the four-to-five-slot migration explicit: current JS and Clojure contexts contain only `:handlers`. Distinguish frame count `n` from activation depth. The P4 description should not claim existing PHP generator rebasing matches Python: PHP currently restores its saved context directly; Python recalculates the crossing base.\n\n**HIGH | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:305](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:305) | Descriptor hooks introduce lexical capture and insufficient authority validation | Make generated references hygienic and validate exact authorized exports.**\n\nCurrent Python lowering deliberately emits protected qualified runtime names. Replacing collection construction with bare `conj` permits a valid Python parameter named `conj` to capture generated machinery. Alias resolution also needs a defined precedence relative to locals, `nonlocal`, globals, and ambient defaults.\n\nNamespace coverage in §6.3 proves neither export existence nor permission to expose it. The `yrt.*` provenance prohibition does not itself stop a descriptor alias or source-visible projection from exposing reserved `cell/dsg__*` doors. Registry availability is not source-level authority.\n\nRequire hygienic generated references, scope-aware alias resolution, exact export/profile checks, and reserved-door restrictions for source-facing projections.\n\nThe bundled Clojure wrapper needs a precise helper plan too. The existing preamble supplies four named helpers, and source `conj`/`vec` shadowing is already refused; the document’s claim that all listed primitives have existing `dsg__*` replacements is inaccurate. New helpers must remain portable guest closures, not captured host functions.\n\n**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:169](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:169) | Float extraction assumes equivalence that live implementations do not have | Preserve evaluation order or explicitly approve a semantic change.**\n\nPython/JS exponentiation recursively computes a half-power and squares its result. PHP squares the base before recursion. These orders round differently.\n\nAn in-memory reproduction of the source algorithms for `1.1 ** 6` produced:\n\n| Algorithm | Binary64 result |\n|---|---|\n| Python/JS | `0x1.c585058dde7acp+0` |\n| PHP | `0x1.c585058dde7abp+0` |\n\nSee [Python fpow:1514](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1514) and [PHP pow-sq:1361](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:1361). One shared implementation cannot preserve both results unchanged.\n\nSpecify signed underflow/overflow behavior, NaN and infinity preconditions, and the finite-input domain of `fmod-pos`. Reinstate the explicit no-capture/no-safepoint rule around unwrapped arithmetic, including policy-hook paths. Exported constants and retained values must preserve float64 kind.\n\n**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:181](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:181) | The integer adapter misstates the refusal algebra | Preserve returned reasons separately from operational failures.**\n\nInteger v4 returns keyword reasons for bit limits, digit limits, and float overflow. Other refusals throw operational failures; they are not structured result maps for a generic guest handler. This is explicit in [yin.vm.integer:81](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:81).\n\nThe proposed signature omits a dedicated float-overflow policy and leaves `on-refusal` ambiguous. PHP’s `int-result` also performs int-range-to-float conversion rather than the same translation as Python/JS.\n\nDefine successful result shapes and all three returned reasons precisely. Do not catch and relabel wrong-type, arity, or invalid-kernel-operation defects as guest exceptions. Keep coercion and whitespace policy language-owned; a shared `skip-ws` needs an explicit character policy.\n\n**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:196](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:196) | The profile schema does not yet establish the claimed complete, reproducible composition | Separate emission recipes from resolved runtime profiles.**\n\nThe illustrated profile pins component addresses but contains no address for the owning prelude’s code. Names, revision labels, and emitter options alone do not content-pin its definition vector.\n\nSimilarly, a list of component names/addresses is insufficient to validate transitive dependencies, exports, or cycles without the corresponding artifacts. Existing `prelude-admission` checks host availability and constructs specifications; it does not supply that artifact closure.\n\nSpecify an immutable, verified artifact set as validation input, pin the root runtime module, and reconcile component edges with dependency manifests. Check collisions by qualified bindings and explicit source projections; equal bare export names in distinct modules are legitimate.\n\nClarify whether `:effects` is an allowed-effect ceiling. If it merely repeats the footprint already derived by publication, omit the duplicate authority. A ceiling must be checked against the derived transitive footprint.\n\n**MEDIUM | [docs/design/yang.preludes.md:459](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.preludes.md:459) | Migration promises and acceptance gates need reconciliation | Define address-neutral boundaries and phase-specific evidence.**\n\nP3 promises zero changes to existing languages, while D6 proposes making `yang.clojure` itself consume the new standalone library. Replacing current `library-for` bundling changes code and dependency identities. Keep existing defaults intact or schedule that migration as an explicit additional profile change.\n\nP0 must preserve existing sequencing, initialization placement, tail marking, and Clojure’s custom preamble—not only linear versus balanced emission. Section 6.3 also calls lowering parameterization P0, whereas the roadmap assigns it to P2.\n\nThe gates need these additions:\n\n| Phase | Required clarification |\n|---|---|\n| P0 | Compare against frozen pre-change artifacts; test verified dependency closure, missing exports, privilege exclusions, and actual projected-name collisions. |\n| P1 | Test existing generators, five-slot migration, terminal exits, cleanup replacement, numeric bit patterns, no-capture float seams, and independent importing tasks. |\n| P2 | Cover every supported lowering path and capability mode, including `:cell` and `:ambient-only`; generator expressions must not evade generator refusal. |\n| P3 | Test separate-unit admission, lexical shadowing, primitive projections, module/global isolation, outcome-shaped ordinary data, and the portable library subset. |\n| P4 | Preserve PHP lifecycle policy and distinguish its context behavior from Python rebasing. |\n\n**3. Confirmed passing**\n\nThe following architectural choices are sound, subject to the corrections above:\n\n- **Explicit state ownership:** Shared escape code receiving `ctx-cell` avoids a shared runtime handler slot. Language/task-owned cells fit the existing heap model. This establishes the intended boundary, not yet tested isolation.\n- **Explicit control and guest closures:** `%capture`, saved context, and ordinary guest continuations preserve explicit causality. Policy closures do not introduce host callbacks.\n- **Appropriate extraction boundaries:** Keeping object systems, coercions, import lifecycles, PHP reference management, and PHP budget hooks outside generic shared machinery preserves cohesion.\n- **Escape-policy separation:** `try-orelse` versus `try-value`, normal-finally arguments, empty-stack policy, and PHP’s abandon-without-finally path correctly reflect real differences.\n- **Provenance placement:** Excluding mint/read privileges from `yrt.*` is correct. The VM’s immutable private provenance sibling and collector tracing support retained-condition lifetime without an ambient registry. Enforcement remains incomplete as described above.\n- **Open catalog compatibility:** No `yang.frontend` schema change is inherently required. Its immutable catalog, explicit pins, and ambiguity refusal support this design. Distinct configurations must receive distinct immutable revisions: identical `[id revision]` with different profiles correctly refuses.\n- **Content-addressing discipline:** Schema-1 manifests, external C1 tables, explicit emitter shape, canonical operand construction, and retaining source/UAST are appropriate. Different descriptors need not always produce different UAST addresses; provenance must retain that many-to-one possibility.\n- **Portability preparation:** Consolidating `defquoted` is reasonable if CLJD EDN expansion, macro imports, and reader aliases remain equivalent. Avoiding integral float literals and constructing negative zero multiplicatively follow existing rules.\n- **Malleability:** Separating pure composition, admission, publication, and guest runtimes is useful. Keep frontend-specific hook contracts outside Yang core and measure guest export surface as well as host `defn` counts. No new VM opcode or AST tag is needed.\n\n**4. Assessment of open owner decisions**\n\n| Decision | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| **D1 — Local mutation** | **Choose A: cells.** It matches current Python scope mechanics and supports reassignment and captured locals. Specify unbound reads, parameter binding, `nonlocal`, and per-module globals. Single-assignment should be a separate restricted profile. |\n| **D2 — Module prefix** | **Choose A: `yrt.*`.** Linker names and implementation namespaces may differ; this follows the existing design direction. |\n| **D3 — Python float tagging** | **Choose A: retain tags.** Removing them changes type discrimination, equality/coercion paths, and observable values. It exceeds a runtime extraction. |\n| **D4 — Batched bump** | **Choose A conditionally.** Validate each extraction independently, then activate one coordinated profile bump. Golden-refresh convenience must not justify unreviewed numerical changes. |\n| **D5 — Identifier mapping** | **Choose A: curated aliases.** Require scope-aware resolution, exact authorized targets, primitive callable projections, and collision tests. Table uniqueness alone is insufficient. |\n| **D6 — Standalone library** | **Choose a constrained A.** Publish a defined portable surface with explicit dependencies and per-task instantiation. Preserve existing default Clojure emission for P3’s zero-churn promise; migrate it separately. Copying library implementations into `clj.py` would create unnecessary maintenance divergence. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
