{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '2529,2600p' && nl -ba src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | sed -n '1272,1308p' && rg -n \\\"\\\\[py/fn|recur|defn.*lambda\\\" src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"  2529\t#### 8.5.5 Float addresses\n  2530\t\n  2531\tThis section records the float-address ruling and the converged sign-off\n  2532\trulings that followed it. The float-fix slice implements them, as the one\n  2533\tpreparatory slice that lands before any float-bearing cross-host pin;\n  2534\t\"Implementation\" below records what it does.\n  2535\t\n  2536\tThe codec conforms; the producers are defective. On JavaScript,\n  2537\t`dao.jing.cbor` classifies an integral Number other than negative zero\n  2538\tas an integer, exactly as `dao.jing.cbor.md` (*Numeric identity*)\n  2539\trequires: JavaScript callers must use the float64 carrier for integral\n  2540\tfloats. The Python producers lose the float kind before encoding:\n  2541\t\n  2542\t- Source literals. Lowering wraps a bare host number as\n  2543\t  `{:py/float v}`. The wrapper carries Python type semantics only; Jing\n  2544\t  classifies the nested number by host, so `2.0` is integer 2 on Node\n  2545\t  and float64 on JVM and Dart.\n  2546\t- Prelude constants. The quoted prelude holds integral float literals\n  2547\t  such as `1.0`, which the ClojureScript reader collapses to `1` before\n  2548\t  any code runs.\n  2549\t- Runtime values. `py/float` builds `{:py/float (* 1.0 x)}`, so `4/2` is\n  2550\t  `{:py/float 2}` on Node, and any addressed image holding it diverges.\n  2551\t- The loud variant: an integral float at or above 2^53 hits Node's\n  2552\t  unsafe-integer refusal.\n  2553\t\n  2554\tBoth rejected alternatives fail for the same reason, that the kind is\n  2555\tgone before the codec sees the number. Normalizing integral floats to\n  2556\tintegers already describes Node; extending it to JVM and Dart violates\n  2557\t\"`1` and `1.0` have different addresses\". Tagging row inputs alone\n  2558\tleaves runtime values diverging and gives Node two representations,\n  2559\tsince a JVM-minted float row already decodes to the carrier on Node.\n  2560\t\n  2561\tThe normative rule, astra's text as widened by fable's correction 1 to\n  2562\truntime values:\n  2563\t\n  2564\t> Every floating-point value entering canonical rows or any addressed\n  2565\t> image, including source literals, prelude constants, nested Python\n  2566\t> float payloads, and runtime float values held by snapshots and\n  2567\t> continuations, must preserve float64 kind before host representation\n  2568\t> erases it. Addressed values use Jing's existing float64\n  2569\t> representation. A Python float wrapper alone does not establish the\n  2570\t> numeric kind of its payload. Row projection and transformation must\n  2571\t> preserve that kind.\n  2572\t\n  2573\tThe mechanism is Jing's existing `dao.jing/float64` carrier (tag 27),\n  2574\tinserted at the producer, under these constraints:\n  2575\t\n  2576\t- One representation. Every `:py/float` payload is Jing float64 content,\n  2577\t  `cbor/float64` of the value: the identity on JVM and Dart, the carrier\n  2578\t  on JavaScript. On JavaScript every payload is a carrier, non-integral\n  2579\t  values included, since a carrier equals only another carrier.\n  2580\t- Kind is fixed where it is still known. Lowering constructs the carrier\n  2581\t  while literal syntax still identifies a float; the prelude marks its\n  2582\t  float constants explicitly before JavaScript collapses them. A later\n  2583\t  scan of bare numbers cannot recover the distinction.\n  2584\t- The prelude seam. `py/float` wraps and `py/num` unwraps through two\n  2585\t  new pure functions in the `data` module's table (section 9.3);\n  2586\t  arithmetic between them stays host-native on bare numbers. The quoted\n  2587\t  prelude carries no integral float literal: `(* 1.0 x)` coercions and\n  2588\t  constants such as `1.0` and `0.0` go through the data functions, while\n  2589\t  a non-integral constant such as `0.5` stays. A JVM test fails on any\n  2590\t  integral-valued double in a prelude literal row.\n  2591\t- No capture inside the seam: no yield or safepoint sits between an\n  2592\t  unwrap and its rewrap. Whether the safepoint stage marks sites inside\n  2593\t  prelude bodies is unverified, so a test pins this.\n  2594\t- No new tag, payload kind, or row shape. Tag 27, signed-zero\n  2595\t  preservation, NaN normalization, and the refusals of equal-value,\n  2596\t  different-kind collection collisions are unchanged. The generic codec\n  2597\t  learns no Python map semantics; its canonical encoding contract and\n  2598\t  frozen fixtures are not edited. The converged refusal ruling amends\n  2599\t  this for the carrier's behavior only (below).\n  2600\t\n  1272\t                     (u/app (u/v body) (u/app (u/v 'data/nth) (u/v invocation) (u/lit 1)))\n  1273\t                     forged-refusal)\n  1274\t                   forged-refusal)\n  1275\t                 entry-body)\n  1276\t        entry (u/lam [invocation] packet)]\n  1277\t    (u/lam [body] entry)))\n  1278\t\n  1279\t\n  1280\t(defn- admit-call-descriptor\n  1281\t  \"Admit a descriptor constructed by trusted UAST/profile code. This is not\n  1282\t   a source form: entry-spelled source maps never pass through this constructor.\"\n  1283\t  [descriptor-ast tail?]\n  1284\t  (let [descriptor-name 'dsg__descriptor\n  1285\t        canonical (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) descriptor-ast\n  1286\t                         (u/lit :clj/admission) (u/lit nil))\n  1287\t        ;; The factory closure captures the canonical descriptor and private\n  1288\t        ;; token. Projection reconstructs and compares that opaque closure;\n  1289\t        ;; it never applies a source-supplied predicate. An equal immutable\n  1290\t        ;; reconstruction is the same value, not admission of different data.\n  1291\t        witness (u/app (u/v 'dsg__make_admission) (u/v descriptor-name))\n  1292\t        published (u/let1 descriptor-name canonical\n  1293\t                          (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/v descriptor-name)\n  1294\t                                 (u/lit :clj/admission) witness))]\n  1295\t    (cond-> (u/mark-tails published)\n  1296\t      tail? (assoc :tail? true))))\n  1297\t\n  1298\t\n  1299\t(defn- compile-call-descriptor\n  1300\t  \"Publish a private body through a canonical constructor-created entry.\"\n  1301\t  [body-ast tail?]\n  1302\t  (admit-call-descriptor\n  1303\t    (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/lit {})\n  1304\t           (u/lit :yang.call/entry) (u/app (u/v 'dsg__make_entry) body-ast)\n  1305\t           (u/lit :clj/type) (u/lit :function))\n  1306\t    tail?))\n  1307\t\n  1308\t\n100:     py.rt/limit     the recursion limit, 1000 by default; outside the\n275:     ;; the recursion-limit cell, the same door\n443:                               {:py/str \"maximum recursion depth exceeded\"})\n793:     ;; and tuples, matched recursively. A target that is neither is a\n965:    [py/fn-error\n3985:                 ;; CPython's default sys.getrecursionlimit()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
